{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785364625670,"sessionID":"ses_04ff9ee89ffe21MRExDwpvIhCN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"vZUOy65mcMTtUF2gyUcjrVAluiSy7F5E","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sh /workspace/.agent_scripts/search.sh \"Transformers architecture self-attention positional encoding encoders decoders\""},"output":"1. The Illustrated Transformer\nhttps://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/\nIn the decoder, the self-attention layer is only allowed to attend to earlier positions in the output sequence. This is done by masking future positions (setting them to -inf) before the softmax step in the self-attention calculation. The “Encoder-Decoder Attention” layer works just like multiheaded self-attention, except it creates its Queries matrix from the layer below it, and takes the Keys and Values matrix from the output of the encoder stack...\n\n2. 11.7. The Transformer Architecture — Dive into Deep Learning 1.0.3 documentation\nhttps://d2l.ai/chapter_attention-mechanisms-and-transformers/transformer.html\n11.7.1. Model As an instance of the encoder-decoder architecture, the overall architecture of the Transformer is presented in Fig. 11.7.1. As we can see, the Transformer is composed of an encoder and a decoder. In contrast to Bahdanau attention for sequence-to-sequence learning in Fig. 11.4.2, the input (source) and output (target) sequence embeddings are added with positional encoding ...\n\n3. The Transformer Architecture: A Deep Dive into Positional ... - Medium\nhttps://medium.com/@sri67ram/the-transformer-architecture-a-deep-dive-into-positional-encodings-attention-mechanisms-313038e51f6f\nThe Transformer Architecture: A Deep Dive into Positional Encodings, Attention Mechanisms, and Encoder-Decoder Design A comprehensive guide for ML practitioners Introduction In 2017, a landmark …\n\n4. How Transformers Work: A Detailed Exploration of Transformer Architecture | DataCamp\nhttps://www.datacamp.com/tutorial/how-transformers-work\nTransformers are neural network architectures that use self-attention mechanisms to process sequential data in parallel, replacing the need for recurrence · Key components: input embeddings, positional encoding, multi-head self-attention, feed-forward networks, and layer normalization with ...\n\n5. Transformer Architecture Deep Dive: How Self-Attention, Positional ...\nhttps://engineersuniverse.com/studios/ai/aie-transformer-architecture-deep-dive\nA thorough technical explanation of the Transformer architecture behind ChatGPT, Claude, and other large language models — covering the encoder-decoder design, multi-head self-attention with Q/K/V matrices, scaled dot-product attention, sinusoidal and RoPE positional encoding, BPE tokenization, and the key differences between GPT and BERT architectures.\n\n6. Transformers without positional encodings.\nhttps://www.reddit.com/r/deeplearning/comments/1ew4dv4/transformers_without_positional_encodings/\nHistorically it was added because the Transformer's Encoder component needed it, otherwise “chicken eats man” and “man eats chicken” would be the same, and the Decoder's translation would vary very differently. ... This has been the crux of problem for me to understand. If the model doesn't inherently know about positions and learns to use pos emb, it can as well do it with attention ... More on reddit.com\n\n7. Transformer Architecture Explained With Self-Attention Mechanism | Codecademy\nhttps://www.codecademy.com/article/transformer-architecture-self-attention-mechanism\nA transformer architecture has ... positional encoding, a multi-head attention layer, an Add & Norm layer, a feed-forward neural network, a masked multi-head attention layer, a linear neural network, and a softmax layer.\n\n8. Transformer Architecture Explained: Self-Attention, Encoders, and Decoders\nhttps://chizkidd.github.io/2026/04/17/transformers/\nTransformer Architecture Explained: Self-Attention, Encoders, and Decoders Apr 17, 2026 • 3 min read Transformers are a sequence-to-sequence model: given an input sequence, produce an output sequence. Architecture: an Encoder processes the input; a Decoder generates the output autoregressively.\n\n9. 11.6. Self-Attention and Positional Encoding — Dive into Deep Learning 1.0.3 documentation\nhttps://d2l.ai/chapter_attention-mechanisms-and-transformers/self-attention-and-positional-encoding.html\nBecause every token is attending to each other token (unlike the case where decoder steps attend to encoder steps), such architectures are typically described as self-attention models (Lin et al., 2017, Vaswani et al., 2017), and elsewhere described as intra-attention model (Cheng et al., 2016, Parikh et al., 2016, Paulus et al., 2017).\n\n10. Complete Guide to Transformer Architecture & Attention Mechanisms | From Theory to Implementation | TensorGym Blog\nhttps://tensorgym.com/blog/transformer-attention\nBeyond this basic implementation, exploring multi-head attention, positional encodings, and the complete transformer architecture with encoder-decoder blocks offers comprehensive understanding of these powerful models that drive today's most advanced AI systems.\n\n","metadata":{"output":"1. The Illustrated Transformer\nhttps://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/\nIn the decoder, the self-attention layer is only allowed to attend to earlier positions in the output sequence. This is done by masking future positions (setting them to -inf) before the softmax step in the self-attention calculation. The “Encoder-Decoder Attention” layer works just like multiheaded self-attention, except it creates its Queries matrix from the layer below it, and takes the Keys and Values matrix from the output of the encoder stack...\n\n2. 11.7. The Transformer Architecture — Dive into Deep Learning 1.0.3 documentation\nhttps://d2l.ai/chapter_attention-mechanisms-and-transformers/transformer.html\n11.7.1. Model As an instance of the encoder-decoder architecture, the overall architecture of the Transformer is presented in Fig. 11.7.1. As we can see, the Transformer is composed of an encoder and a decoder. In contrast to Bahdanau attention for sequence-to-sequence learning in Fig. 11.4.2, the input (source) and output (target) sequence embeddings are added with positional encoding ...\n\n3. The Transformer Architecture: A Deep Dive into Positional ... - Medium\nhttps://medium.com/@sri67ram/the-transformer-architecture-a-deep-dive-into-positional-encodings-attention-mechanisms-313038e51f6f\nThe Transformer Architecture: A Deep Dive into Positional Encodings, Attention Mechanisms, and Encoder-Decoder Design A comprehensive guide for ML practitioners Introduction In 2017, a landmark …\n\n4. How Transformers Work: A Detailed Exploration of Transformer Architecture | DataCamp\nhttps://www.datacamp.com/tutorial/how-transformers-work\nTransformers are neural network architectures that use self-attention mechanisms to process sequential data in parallel, replacing the need for recurrence · Key components: input embeddings, positional encoding, multi-head self-attention, feed-forward networks, and layer normalization with ...\n\n5. Transformer Architecture Deep Dive: How Self-Attention, Positional ...\nhttps://engineersuniverse.com/studios/ai/aie-transformer-architecture-deep-dive\nA thorough technical explanation of the Transformer architecture behind ChatGPT, Claude, and other large language models — covering the encoder-decoder design, multi-head self-attention with Q/K/V matrices, scaled dot-product attention, sinusoidal and RoPE positional encoding, BPE tokenization, and the key differences between GPT and BERT architectures.\n\n6. Transformers without positional encodings.\nhttps://www.reddit.com/r/deeplearning/comments/1ew4dv4/transformers_without_positional_encodings/\nHistorically it was added because the Transformer's Encoder component needed it, otherwise “chicken eats man” and “man eats chicken” would be the same, and the Decoder's translation would vary very differently. ... This has been the crux of problem for me to understand. If the model doesn't inherently know about positions and learns to use pos emb, it can as well do it with attention ... More on reddit.com\n\n7. Transformer Architecture Explained With Self-Attention Mechanism | Codecademy\nhttps://www.codecademy.com/article/transformer-architecture-self-attention-mechanism\nA transformer architecture has ... positional encoding, a multi-head attention layer, an Add & Norm layer, a feed-forward neural network, a masked multi-head attention layer, a linear neural network, and a softmax layer.\n\n8. Transformer Architecture Explained: Self-Attention, Encoders, and Decoders\nhttps://chizkidd.github.io/2026/04/17/transformers/\nTransformer Architecture Explained: Self-Attention, Encoders, and Decoders Apr 17, 2026 • 3 min read Transformers are a sequence-to-sequence model: given an input sequence, produce an output sequence. Architecture: an Encoder processes the input; a Decoder generates the output autoregressively.\n\n9. 11.6. Self-Attention and Positional Encoding — Dive into Deep Learning 1.0.3 documentation\nhttps://d2l.ai/chapter_attention-mechanisms-and-transformers/self-attention-and-positional-encoding.html\nBecause every token is attending to each other token (unlike the case where decoder steps attend to encoder steps), such architectures are typically described as self-attention models (Lin et al., 2017, Vaswani et al., 2017), and elsewhere described as intra-attention model (Cheng et al., 2016, Parikh et al., 2016, Paulus et al., 2017).\n\n10. Complete Guide to Transformer Architecture & Attention Mechanisms | From Theory to Implementation | TensorGym Blog\nhttps://tensorgym.com/blog/transformer-attention\nBeyond this basic implementation, exploring multi-head attention, positional encodings, and the complete transformer architecture with encoder-decoder blocks offers comprehensive understanding of these powerful models that drive t